\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{A probability law on coherent box means}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{Let #{\gamma_B} be the limiting probability laws of
\ref{fdip-01D6}, for all positive integers #{B}. They satisfy
##{(M_{B',B})_*\gamma_B=\gamma_{B'}\qquad(B'\mid B).}
There is a unique Borel probability measure on
##{\mathcal Y=\{(z_B)_{B\ge1}:z_B\in\RR^{(\ZZ /B)^n},
\quad z_{B'}=M_{B',B}z_B\text{ whenever }B'\mid B\}}
whose #{B}-coordinate has law #{\gamma_B} for every #{B}.
The space #{\mathcal Y}, with its topology inherited from the
countable product of these finite-dimensional spaces, is their
\vocabk{inverse limit}{tt-002D} under merging. A coherent assignment of box means
is a point of this space; the probability is a measure on it.}

\proof{
\p{For the [box and merging maps](fdip-01D5), every
#{B'}-box is the disjoint union of #{q^n} equal-size #{B}-boxes,
where #{q=B/B'}. Averaging over this partition gives
#{A_{B',N}=M_{B',B}A_{B,N}}. For #{B''\mid B'\mid B},
partitioning once more gives
#{M_{B'',B'}M_{B',B}=M_{B'',B}}; also #{M_{B,B}=\bbI}.
Fix #{B'\mid B} and take #{N\to\infty} through multiples
of #{B}. The first identity passes to the
limits of \ref{fdip-01D6}, since merging is a fixed continuous
linear map. This proves consistency.}

\p{The chain #{B_j=j!}, for #{j\ge1}, is cofinal: every
positive integer #{B} divides #{j!} for all #{j\ge B}.
Apply the countable projective probability construction used in
\ref{fdip-01CN} to the consistent laws #{\gamma_{j!}} and
their successive merging maps. It gives a probability on coherent
factorial coordinates. From such a sequence define #{z_B} by
merging from any factorial coordinate divisible by #{B}.
Choosing a larger factorial does not change the result, by
coherence and composition of merging. Each new coordinate is a
continuous linear image of a fixed factorial coordinate, so this
defines a measurable map into the full countable product. Its
image is in #{\mathcal Y}, and its #{B}-coordinate has law
#{\gamma_B}.}

\p{The coherence equations define closed subsets of the product;
their countable intersection is #{\mathcal Y}. Finally, every
finite set of box coordinates is determined, on this space, by
the coordinate at a common multiple of their sizes. Any probability
with the prescribed single-coordinate laws therefore has the same
finite joint laws. Cylinder sets generate the Borel sigma-algebra
of the product and of its closed subspace, proving uniqueness.}
}

\p{These partitions have rational coordinate boundaries. Every
finite collection of rational coordinate boundaries belongs to
a sufficiently fine factorial partition. Powers of two alone
are not cofinal in this system: no such power is divisible by
#{3}. They define a restricted system, rather than the full
system used here.}
